Instructions
  •  Write function definitions (including main) and complete C program code for each question.
  • From Standard C Library Functions, you can only use the ones listed in “Worksheet_C1.pdf” if the
     library is included.
  • Do not include any additional libraries. Do not use recursions.
  • Do not define constant/global variables. You must not corrupt the data.
  • You must follow declaration/loop restrictions to get points from a part.
  • You can assume that parameters are always passed correctly by the user.
  • You can assume that arrays have at least one element.
  • You can assume that the lengths of the arrays are sufficient.
  • You can assume that strings end with the null character as expected.
  • Do not use integer value of a char directly. For example, do not use 65 for ‘A’.

Question 1
#include <stdio.h>
#define MAX_SIZE 500

void swap(int *x, int *y);
// The function exchanges the values of integers pointed by x and y.
// You are allowed to make only 1 variable declaration.

void isSorted(int *A, int *p);
// The function checks if integer array A is sorted (in ascending order) or not.
// You can assume that there is always 0 (as stop character) at the end of the array.
/* If A is sorted, the function sets the integer pointed by p to 1. Otherwise, it sets the integer pointed by
p to 0. */
// You are not allowed to make any variable declarations.

void indexOfMin(int *A, int *p);
/* The function finds the minimum value of the positive integer array A and saves its index (starting from
0) to the integer pointed by p. You can assume that there is always 0 (as stop character) at the end of
the array. */
// You are allowed to make only 1 pointer declaration.

int main(int argc, char *argv[]);
// The function creates an integer array A with size MAX_SIZE.
// It gets positive integers from the user one by one and saves them to array A until the user enters 0.
// It also adds 0 to the end of the array as stopping character.
// Then, it checks if A is sorted (in ascending order) or not using isSorted function.
// If A is not sorted, it sorts A using the below sorting algorithm and functions indexOfMin, swap.
// The function prints A as a line before exit.
// Space complexity of the sorting algorithm must be O(1) meaning that you cannot use additional arrays.
// You are allowed to use at most 3 loops.

Sorting Algorithm:
Let N be the number of elements in A excluding 0 (at the end).
FOR i = 0 to (N-2)
       Find index of min element of subarray of A starting from i-th element to the end of A using indexOfMin.
      Swap the minimum element of the subarray with i-th element of A using swap function.

